{
  "id": "about-the-author",
  "kind": "epilogue",
  "sourceKind": "about-author",
  "label": "About the Author",
  "title": "ABOUT THE AUTHOR",
  "shortTitle": "ABOUT THE AUTHOR",
  "sourceRange": [
    1830,
    1847
  ],
  "headerSourceParagraphs": {
    "title": 1830
  },
  "elements": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "Lalit is a student and writer who explores human consciousness not as a concept, but as a lived disturbance."
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1831
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "His work does not attempt to explain life."
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1832
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "It observes what happens when life starts getting observed too closely."
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1833
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "He is interested in the subtle breaking point where awareness stops being clarity and starts becoming overload where understanding no longer simplifies experience, but starts separating the experiencer from the experience itself."
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1834
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "Most of his writing moves through the invisible shifts inside the human mind:"
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1835
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "when instinct turns into analysis,"
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1836
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "when feeling turns into interpretation,"
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1837
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "and when presence slowly gets replaced by observation."
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1838
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "He does not see confusion as lack of understanding."
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1839
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "He sees it as the side effect of too much understanding without grounding."
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1840
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "There are no conclusions in his writing because he does not believe human perception ever reaches a final state."
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1841
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "It only keeps restructuring itself depending on how closely it is watched."
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1842
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "This book is not a message."
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1843
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "It is a record of what happens when a mind begins to notice its own patterns without interruption."
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1844
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "And once that noticing begins…"
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1845
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "it rarely fully stops."
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1846
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "It only changes its form."
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1847
    }
  ],
  "suppressHeaderNumber": true,
  "index": 24,
  "file": "chapters/24-about-the-author.json"
}
